tls (SChannel): a certificate the caller trusts by itself, and which one was turned down

What CeauKit's Patches/ffmpeg/0012 does for Secure Transport, for
SChannel, with the same option and the same entry, so that the engine
asks and is told the same on Windows.

A media server on a home network shows a certificate nothing vouches
for but itself. The handshake fails, as it should for a stranger. A
person who has been shown that certificate and said "this is my server"
has no way to be heard: the choices were to verify every certificate
against the system's roots or to verify none.

With the `trusted_cert_sha256` option a caller names one certificate by
the SHA-256 of it as it is sent, in hexadecimal, colons or not. A peer
that shows exactly that certificate is taken as it is, whatever else is
the matter with it (no root, run out, written for another name): the
peer holds its key, and that certificate is what was trusted. Any other
certificate is judged against the system's roots and the name, so a
server with a certificate the system does trust still connects, and a
different one that nothing vouches for is still turned down.

So that a caller can ask somebody whether to trust a certificate at
all, a handshake that fails over the certificate says which one it was:
the same digest, as `refused_cert_sha256` among the options the caller
gets back from the opening that failed.

To do either, a client that verifies no longer has SChannel judge the
server's certificate inside the handshake (SCH_CRED_AUTO_CRED_
VALIDATION), where the certificate cannot be looked at first and a
refusal names none. It judges it itself, when a handshake is through
and before anything of the caller's is sent, and again after every
handshake a server asks for later: the certificate the caller trusts is
taken there, and any other is handed to what Windows judges a server's
certificate with (CertGetCertificateChain, then
CertVerifyCertificateChainPolicy with the rules for a TLS server and
the name asked for). A certificate turned down there is a failed
handshake with the code for it, FFERRTAG(0xF8,'C','R','T').

One thing is not as SChannel had it. SChannel was told to check every
certificate of the chain for revocation and to fail when that could not
be learned, which asks the certificate authority's server over the
network during the handshake. Secure Transport and OpenSSL as Ceau
builds them wait for no such server, and a source that opens on an
iPhone has to open on a PC whose network does not reach it. Here
revocation is read from what Windows already has cached, a certificate
known to be revoked is turned down, and one whose state is not known is
not.

This is code that decides which servers are trusted, written on a Mac
and compiled for Windows. It has not run. What its first run on Windows
has to show is in docs/Engine-Libs.md.

